# Implementation Plan - Security, Performance, and MCQ Improvement Plan

This plan addresses security vulnerabilities in row-level security (RLS) policies, tracks database triggers and indexes formally in migration files, implements missing support for MCQ assignments (rendering and server-side validation), and outlines a verification and testing strategy.

---

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **1. RLS Policy Tighter Restrictions**
> - Currently, any student can approve their own course enrollments because the RLS policy for `enrollments` allows `FOR ALL` modifications using `auth.uid() = user_id`. We will restrict updates to only allow `progress` and `completed_at` to be modified by the student, while reserving `status` updates for admins and instructors.
>
> **2. Proper Security Triggers in migrations**
> - The profile triggers (`protect_profile_fields`) and submission triggers (`protect_submission_fields`) are currently in a manual node script (`inject_security_triggers.js`) which is not part of the `supabase/migrations` folder. This leads to drift. We will move these triggers to a migration file (`012_security_triggers.sql`) and fix the bugs in their logic.
> - Specifically, the trigger previously used `auth.role() = 'authenticated'` which blocked even admin users (who also have authentication role `'authenticated'`) from modifying student roles or approving submissions. We will correct the logic to verify `profiles.role` instead.
>
> **3. MCQ Auto-Approval Vulnerability**
> - MCQ type assignments are auto-approved. However, the UI does not render options, and the server-side action (`submitAssignment`) has no verification of the user's answer. Students can get free XP by submitting blank answers.
> - We will implement MCQ question rendering in `AssignmentCard.tsx` and add server-side validation in `submitAssignment` to verify that the answer index matches the assignment's `correct_index` before auto-approving and awarding XP.

